// Class 12 Chemistry Unit VII: Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers (Official Syllabus - 06 Marks)
// Comprehensive Deep Reference Book Standard (O.P. Tandon / Pradeep's Standard)

export const c12Chem7HtmlOverview = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY CARD -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">Quick Glossary &amp; Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Core Concepts &amp; Key Definitions &bull; Unit VII: Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers (Official Syllabus - 06 Marks)</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">1. Alcohols, Phenols &amp; Ethers:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><b>Alcohols (R-OH)</b> are hydroxy derivatives of aliphatic hydrocarbons where -OH is bonded to an sp<sup>3</sup> hybridised carbon. <b>Phenols (Ar-OH)</b> are hydroxy derivatives of aromatic hydrocarbons where -OH is bonded directly to an sp<sup>2</sup> hybridised carbon of an aromatic benzene ring. <b>Ethers (R-O-R&apos; / Ar-O-R)</b> are dialkyl/diaryl derivatives of water or monoalkyl derivatives of alcohols/phenols.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">2. Lucas Reagent &amp; Lucas Test:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">An equimolar mixture of concentrated HCl and anhydrous ZnCl<sub>2</sub>. Used to distinguish 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; alcohols based on the rate of formation of insoluble alkyl chloride turbidity (3&deg;: immediate turbidity; 2&deg;: turbidity in 5 minutes; 1&deg;: no turbidity at room temperature, appears only upon heating).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">3. Acidic Nature of Phenol &amp; Phenoxide Resonance:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Phenol is significantly more acidic (pK<sub>a</sub> &approx; 10) than aliphatic alcohols (pK<sub>a</sub> &approx; 16-18) because the conjugate base, <b>phenoxide ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>)</b>, is resonance-stabilized with delocalization of negative charge over ortho and para positions of the benzene ring without charge separation.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">4. Kolbe&apos;s Reaction:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Treatment of sodium phenoxide with carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) at 400 K and 4-7 atm pressure followed by acidification yields <b>Salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid)</b> as the major product.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">5. Reimer-Tiemann Reaction:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Reaction of phenol with chloroform (CHCl<sub>3</sub>) in the presence of aqueous NaOH at 340 K introduces an aldehyde group (-CHO) at the ortho position, forming <b>Salicylaldehyde (2-hydroxybenzaldehyde)</b> via the electrophilic intermediate <b>dichlorocarbene (:CCl<sub>2</sub>)</b>.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">6. Williamson Ether Synthesis:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">An S<sub>N</sub>2 reaction between an alkyl halide (R-X) and sodium alkoxide (R&apos;-O<sup>-</sup>Na<sup>+</sup>) to produce symmetrical or asymmetrical ethers. Best yields are obtained when the alkyl halide is primary (1&deg;); tertiary (3&deg;) alkyl halides yield alkenes exclusively via E2 elimination.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 14px 16px; border-left: 4px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">7. Denatured Alcohol (Methylated Spirit):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">Commercial ethyl alcohol made unfit for drinking by the addition of poisonous methanol (about 5-10%), pyridine (for foul smell), and copper sulphate (for blue colour).</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">1. Nomenclature &amp; Classification of Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Classification of Alcohols &amp; Phenols based on Number of -OH Groups</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Monohydric Alcohols:</b> Contain 1 -OH group (e.g. CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH, Ethanol).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Dihydric Alcohols (Glycols):</b> Contain 2 -OH groups (e.g. HO-CH<sub>2</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-OH, Ethane-1,2-diol / Ethylene glycol).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Trihydric Alcohols:</b> Contain 3 -OH groups (e.g. CH<sub>2</sub>OH-CHOH-CH<sub>2</sub>OH, Propane-1,2,3-triol / Glycerol).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Monohydric Phenols:</b> e.g. C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH (Phenol / Carbolic acid), o-Cresol (2-Methylphenol).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Dihydric Phenols:</b> Benzene-1,2-diol (Catechol), Benzene-1,3-diol (Resorcinol), Benzene-1,4-diol (Quinol / Hydroquinone).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Trihydric Phenols:</b> Benzene-1,2,3-triol (Pyrogallol), Benzene-1,3,5-triol (Phloroglucinol).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Classification of Alcohols based on Hybridisation of Carbon Bonded to -OH</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Primary (1&deg;), Secondary (2&deg;) &amp; Tertiary (3&deg;) Alcohols (sp<sup>3</sup> C-OH):</b> -OH bonded to 1&deg;, 2&deg;, or 3&deg; carbon atom.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Allylic Alcohols (sp<sup>3</sup> C-OH):</b> -OH attached to an sp<sup>3</sup> carbon adjacent to a C=C double bond (e.g. CH<sub>2</sub>=CH-CH<sub>2</sub>OH, Prop-2-en-1-ol / Allyl alcohol).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Benzylic Alcohols (sp<sup>3</sup> C-OH):</b> -OH attached to an sp<sup>3</sup> carbon adjacent to an aromatic benzene ring (e.g. C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH, Benzyl alcohol / Phenylmethanol).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Vinylic Alcohols (sp<sup>2</sup> C-OH):</b> -OH bonded directly to an sp<sup>2</sup> carbon of a C=C double bond (e.g. CH<sub>2</sub>=CH-OH, Ethenol / Vinyl alcohol &mdash; rapidly tautomerises to acetaldehyde).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">3. Classification of Ethers (Symmetrical vs Asymmetrical)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Simple / Symmetrical Ethers:</b> Both alkyl or aryl groups attached to the oxygen atom are identical (e.g. C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>: Diethyl ether / Ethoxyethane; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>: Diphenyl ether).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Mixed / Asymmetrical Ethers:</b> Alkyl or aryl groups attached to oxygen are different (e.g. C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-CH<sub>3</sub>: Ethyl methyl ether / Methoxyethane; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-CH<sub>3</sub>: Anisole / Methoxybenzene).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF007F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">WORKED IUPAC NOMENCLATURE EXAMPLES</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">1. (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-OH:</b> 2-Methylpropan-2-ol (tert-Butyl alcohol).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">2. CH<sub>3</sub>-CH(OH)-CH=CH-CH<sub>3</sub>:</b> Pent-3-en-2-ol (-OH gets numbering priority over double bond).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">3. HO-CH<sub>2</sub>-CH(OH)-CH<sub>2</sub>-OH:</b> Propane-1,2,3-triol (Glycerol).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">4. o-NO<sub>2</sub>-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-OH:</b> 2-Nitrophenol.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">5. CH<sub>3</sub>-O-CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>:</b> 2-Methoxypropane (Isopropyl methyl ether).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">6. C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>:</b> Ethoxybenzene (Phenetole).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">2. Structures of Functional Groups &amp; Bond Angle Anomalies</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.08); border-left: 4.5px solid #FF007F; padding: 14px 18px; border-radius: 8px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">COMPARISON: Bond Angles and C-O Bond Lengths</b>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">1. Bond Angle Variations:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">In Methanol (Alcohols):</b> The C-O-H bond angle is <b>108.9&deg;</b>, which is slightly less than the tetrahedral angle (109&deg;28&apos;). This is due to the <b>repulsion between the two unshared lone pairs of electrons</b> on the oxygen atom.<br>\n      &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">In Phenol:</b> The C-O-H bond angle is <b>109&deg;</b>.<br>\n      &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">In Dimethyl Ether (Ethers):</b> The C-O-C bond angle is <b>111.7&deg;</b>, which is <b>greater than the tetrahedral angle</b>. This notable enlargement is caused by the <b>strong steric repulsive interaction between the two bulky alkyl groups (-CH<sub>3</sub>)</b> attached to the central oxygen atom.<br><br>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">2. C-O Bond Length Variations:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">In Methanol (142 pm):</b> Pure sp<sup>3</sup> C-O single bond.<br>\n      &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">In Phenol (136 pm):</b> The C-O bond length is significantly shorter (136 pm) due to: (1) <b>Partial double bond character</b> arising from the conjugation of the unshared electron pair of oxygen with the aromatic benzene ring, and (2) The oxygen is attached to an <b>sp<sup>2</sup> hybridised carbon</b> of benzene, which has greater s-character and is more electronegative than sp<sup>3</sup> carbon.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">3. Methods of Preparation of Alcohols</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Preparation from Alkenes (Hydration vs Hydroboration-Oxidation)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; (a) Acid-Catalysed Hydration (Markovnikov Addition):</b><br>\n        Alkenes react with water in the presence of dilute H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> to form alcohols according to <b>Markovnikov&apos;s Rule</b>:\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">CH<sub>3</sub>-CH=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>-CH(OH)-CH<sub>3</sub> (Propan-2-ol)</div>\n        <i>Mechanism proceeds via a planar carbocation intermediate (susceptible to 1,2-hydride or methyl shifts).</i><br><br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; (b) Hydroboration-Oxidation (Anti-Markovnikov Overall Hydration):</b><br>\n        Diborane (B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>) reacts with alkenes to form trialkylboranes, which on subsequent oxidation with alkaline hydrogen peroxide (H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> / OH<sup>-</sup>) yield primary alcohols with <b>overall Anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry and syn-addition</b>:\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">6 CH<sub>3</sub>-CH=CH<sub>2</sub> + B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> &rarr; 2 (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>3</sub>B &rarr; (3 H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> / OH<sup>-</sup>) &rarr; 6 CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH + 2 H<sub>3</sub>BO<sub>3</sub></div>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Key Advantage:</b> Excellent method for preparing 1&deg; alcohols without carbocation rearrangement!\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Preparation from Carbonyl Compounds (Reduction)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Reduction of Aldehydes &amp; Ketones:</b> Catalytic hydrogenation (H<sub>2</sub>/Ni, Pt, Pd) or hydride transfer agents (<b>NaBH<sub>4</sub></b> in ethanol, or <b>LiAlH<sub>4</sub></b> in dry ether):<br>\n        &bull; Aldehyde (R-CHO) &rarr; <b>Primary alcohol (R-CH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b>.<br>\n        &bull; Ketone (R-CO-R&apos;) &rarr; <b>Secondary alcohol (R-CH(OH)-R&apos;)</b>.<br><br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Reduction of Carboxylic Acids and Esters:</b><br>\n        R-COOH &rarr; (1. LiAlH<sub>4</sub>, 2. H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; R-CH<sub>2</sub>OH.<br>\n        <i>Commercially, acids are first esterified with alcohols and then catalytically hydrogenated using H<sub>2</sub>/catalyst.</i>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">3. Preparation using Grignard Reagent (Nucleophilic Addition)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Grignard reagents (RMgX) undergo nucleophilic addition to carbonyl compounds, followed by acidic hydrolysis:<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">1. Formaldehyde (HCHO) + RMgX &rarr; Primary (1&deg;) Alcohol (R-CH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">2. Other Aldehydes (R&apos;CHO) + RMgX &rarr; Secondary (2&deg;) Alcohol (R&apos;-CH(OH)-R)</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">3. Ketones (R&apos;-CO-R&apos;&apos;) + RMgX &rarr; Tertiary (3&deg;) Alcohol (R&apos;-C(OH)(R)-R&apos;&apos;)</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">4. Physical &amp; Chemical Properties of Alcohols (Mechanism of Dehydration)</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Physical Properties (Boiling Point &amp; Solubility)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; High Boiling Points:</b> Alcohols have much higher boiling points than isomeric ethers, haloalkanes, and hydrocarbons of comparable molecular mass due to the presence of <b>intermolecular Hydrogen Bonding</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Branching Effect:</b> Boiling points decrease with branching among isomeric alcohols (1&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 3&deg;) due to decrease in surface area and London dispersion forces.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Solubility in Water:</b> Lower alcohols are completely miscible in water in all proportions because they form hydrogen bonds with water molecules.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Acidity of Alcohols (Order: 1&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 3&deg;)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Alcohols react with active metals (Na, K, Al) to liberate hydrogen gas, acting as Bronsted acids:<br>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">2 R-OH + 2 Na &rarr; 2 R-O<sup>-</sup>Na<sup>+</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>&uarr;</div>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Relative Acidic Strength:</b> <b>CH<sub>3</sub>OH &gt; 1&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 3&deg;</b>.<br>\n        <i>Reason: Alkyl groups are electron-releasing (+I effect). In 3&deg; alcohols, three alkyl groups pump electron density towards oxygen, destabilizing the alkoxide ion and decreasing the polarity of the O-H bond.</i>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- STEP-BY-STEP DEHYDRATION MECHANISM BOX -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin: 20px 0; box-shadow: 0 4px 20px rgba(0,0,0,0.4);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FF007F; font-weight: bold; font-size: 16.5px; margin-bottom: 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">\n      STEP-BY-STEP BOARD MECHANISM: Acid-Catalysed Dehydration of Ethanol to Ethene (at 443 K)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n      When ethanol is heated with concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (or H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub>) at <b>443 K</b>, it undergoes dehydration to form ethene via a 3-step mechanism:<br><br>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Step 1: Protonation of Alcohol (Fast Step)</b><br>\n      The unshared electron pair on oxygen attacks the proton released by the acid:<br>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-OH + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> (Protonated alcohol / Ethyl oxonium ion)</div>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Step 2: Formation of Carbocation (Slow, Rate-Determining Step - RDS)</b><br>\n      The C-O bond breaks with departure of a neutral water molecule, generating an ethyl carbocation:<br>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (Slow, RDS) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>-C<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O</div>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Step 3: Elimination of Proton to form Ethene (Fast Step)</b><br>\n      A base (H<sub>2</sub>O) abstracts a &beta;-proton from the carbocation, creating the C=C &pi;-bond and regenerating H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>:<br>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 12px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">H-CH<sub>2</sub>-C<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup></div>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Ease of Dehydration Order:</b> <b>3&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg;</b> (governed by the relative stability of intermediate carbocations: 3&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg;).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">5. Identification &amp; Distinction of Primary, Secondary &amp; Tertiary Alcohols</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Lucas Test (conc. HCl + anhydrous ZnCl<sub>2</sub>)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Tertiary (3&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Reacts immediately; <b>turbidity appears instantly</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Secondary (2&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Reacts slowly; <b>turbidity appears within 5 minutes</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Primary (1&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Does not react at room temperature; <b>solution remains clear</b> (turbidity appears only upon heating).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Victor Meyer Test (The \"Red-Blue-White\" Trick)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Sequence: Alcohol &rarr; (P + I<sub>2</sub>) &rarr; Alkyl Iodide &rarr; (AgNO<sub>2</sub>) &rarr; Nitroalkane &rarr; (HNO<sub>2</sub>) &rarr; Product &rarr; (aq. NaOH):<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Primary (1&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Forms Nitrolic acid &rarr; gives <b>Blood-Red colouration</b> with NaOH.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Secondary (2&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Forms Pseudonitrol &rarr; gives <b>Deep Blue colouration</b> with NaOH.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Tertiary (3&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Has no &alpha;-hydrogen &rarr; does not react with HNO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; gives <b>Colourless / White solution</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">3. Catalytic Dehydrogenation with Heated Copper at 573 K</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Primary (1&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Undergoes dehydrogenation to form an <b>Aldehyde</b> (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CHO + H<sub>2</sub>&uarr;).<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Secondary (2&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Undergoes dehydrogenation to form a <b>Ketone</b> (CH<sub>3</sub>-CH(OH)-CH<sub>3</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>&uarr;).<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Tertiary (3&deg;) Alcohol:</b> Has no &alpha;-hydrogen, so it resists dehydrogenation and undergoes <b>Dehydration to yield an Alkene</b> ((CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-OH &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">6. Important Commercial Alcohols: Methanol &amp; Ethanol</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Methanol (Wood Spirit, CH<sub>3</sub>OH)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Industrial Synthesis:</b> CO(g) + 2 H<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; (ZnO - Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> / 573-673 K / 200-300 atm) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>OH(l).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Toxicity:</b> Methanol is metabolized by liver alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde and formic acid, causing blindness and death.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Ethanol (Grain Alcohol, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH) &amp; Fermentation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Fermentation:</b> Sucrose &rarr; (Invertase) &rarr; Glucose + Fructose &rarr; (Zymase) &rarr; 2 C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 2 CO<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Rectified Spirit:</b> 95.6% ethanol + 4.4% water.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Absolute Alcohol:</b> 100% pure ethanol.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Denatured Alcohol:</b> Rendered unfit for drinking by adding methanol, pyridine, and copper sulphate.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">7. Phenols: Preparation &amp; Resonance Origin of Acidic Nature</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Industrial Preparation from Cumene (Cumene Hydroperoxide Method)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Cumene (isopropylbenzene) is oxidized with air to <b>cumene hydroperoxide</b>, which on acidic hydrolysis yields <b>Phenol and Acetone</b> in high yield:<br>\n        <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px 14px; text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; font-size: 15px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>-O-O-H &rarr; (dil. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Resonance Explanation for Acidity of Phenol</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Phenol is stronger acid than alcohols because the conjugate base, <b>phenoxide ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>)</b>, is resonance-stabilized with negative charge delocalized over the ortho and para positions of the benzene ring. Phenoxide ion has no charge separation, whereas resonance structures of un-ionized phenol possess charge separation (+ on oxygen, - on ring).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Substituent Effects:</b> Electron-withdrawing groups (-NO<sub>2</sub>) at ortho and para positions increase acidity (Picric acid &gt; p-nitrophenol &gt; o-nitrophenol &gt; m-nitrophenol &gt; phenol); Electron-donating groups (-CH<sub>3</sub>) decrease acidity.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">8. Chemical Reactions of Phenols: Electrophilic Substitution &amp; Named Reactions</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Electrophilic Substitution (Bromination &amp; Nitration)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Bromination in CS<sub>2</sub> (273 K):</b> Yields <b>4-Bromophenol (major)</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Bromine Water (Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O):</b> Yields a white precipitate of <b>2,4,6-Tribromophenol</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Nitration with dil. HNO<sub>3</sub>:</b> Yields <b>o-Nitrophenol &amp; p-Nitrophenol</b> (separable by steam distillation due to intramolecular vs intermolecular H-bonding).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Nitration with conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>:</b> Yields <b>2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (Picric acid)</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Named Reactions &amp; Other Key Reactions of Phenol</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Kolbe&apos;s Reaction:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa + CO<sub>2</sub> (400 K, 4-7 atm) &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; <b>Salicylic acid</b> (used to prepare Aspirin).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Reimer-Tiemann Reaction:</b> Phenol + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + NaOH (340 K) &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; <b>Salicylaldehyde</b> (intermediate: :CCl<sub>2</sub> dichlorocarbene).<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Reaction with Zinc Dust:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + Zn &rarr; (Heat) &rarr; <b>Benzene (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>)</b> + ZnO.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Oxidation:</b> Phenol + Na<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>p-Benzoquinone</b>.<br>\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Neutral FeCl<sub>3</sub> Test:</b> Gives a characteristic <b>violet colouration</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 9 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">9. Ethers: Preparation, Physical Properties &amp; Williamson Synthesis Rules</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Preparation from Alcohols (413 K vs 443 K)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        &bull; At <b>413 K (140 &deg;C)</b> with conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>: 2 C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &rarr; <b>C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> (Diethyl ether)</b> + H<sub>2</sub>O (S<sub>N</sub>2 pathway).<br>\n        &bull; At <b>443 K (170 &deg;C)</b> with conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>: C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &rarr; <b>CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> (Ethene)</b> + H<sub>2</sub>O (E1 elimination).\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Williamson Ether Synthesis &amp; Substrate Rules</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">R-X + R&apos;-ONa &rarr; R-O-R&apos; + NaX</b> (S<sub>N</sub>2 mechanism).<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Rule:</b> The alkyl halide (R-X) <b>must be 1&deg;</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">Trap with 3&deg; Halide:</b> If a 3&deg; alkyl halide is used (e.g. (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-Br + CH<sub>3</sub>ONa), the strongly basic alkoxide causes <b>E2 elimination yielding an Alkene ((CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C=CH<sub>2</sub>) exclusively</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">To prepare (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O-CH<sub>3</sub>:</b> Use <b>CH<sub>3</sub>I (1&deg; halide) + (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O<sup>-</sup>Na<sup>+</sup> (3&deg; alkoxide)</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 10 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">10. Chemical Reactions of Ethers: C-O Cleavage by HI &amp; Substitution in Anisole</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">1. Cleavage of C-O Bond by Concentrated HI</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">With 1&deg; / 2&deg; Alkyl Groups (S<sub>N</sub>2):</b> Iodide attacks the smaller alkyl group &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>-O-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + HI &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>I + CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">With a 3&deg; Alkyl Group (S<sub>N</sub>1):</b> Tertiary carbocation forms preferentially &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O-CH<sub>3</sub> + HI &rarr; <b>(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-I + CH<sub>3</sub>OH</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">With Anisole (Alkyl Aryl Ether):</b> Ar-O bond has partial double bond character &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-CH<sub>3</sub> + HI &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH (Phenol) + CH<sub>3</sub>I</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.35); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">2. Electrophilic Substitution in Anisole</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n        Methoxy group (-OCH<sub>3</sub>) activates the benzene ring and directs electrophiles to ortho and practical positions (para major):<br>\n        &bull; <b>Bromination:</b> Anisole + Br<sub>2</sub>/CH<sub>3</sub>COOH &rarr; <b>4-Bromoanisole (major)</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b>Friedel-Crafts:</b> Alkylation &rarr; <b>4-Methoxytoluene (major)</b>; Acylation &rarr; <b>4-Methoxyacetophenone (major)</b>.<br>\n        &bull; <b>Nitration:</b> Anisole + HNO<sub>3</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>4-Nitroanisole (major)</b>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 11 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; border-bottom: 2px solid #FF007F; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 36px;\">11. Master Revision Formula &amp; Named Reactions Cheat Sheet</h2>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.4); border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">1. Key Reaction Matrix:</b>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Hydration:</b> Alkene + H<sub>2</sub>O/H<sup>+</sup> &rarr; Alcohol (Markovnikov).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Hydroboration-Oxidation:</b> Alkene + B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>/OH<sup>-</sup> &rarr; 1&deg; Alcohol (Anti-Markovnikov).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Cumene to Phenol:</b> Cumene + O<sub>2</sub> &rarr; Hydroperoxide &rarr; Phenol + Acetone.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Kolbe:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa + CO<sub>2</sub> (400 K, 4-7 atm) &rarr; Salicylic acid.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Reimer-Tiemann:</b> Phenol + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + NaOH (340 K) &rarr; Salicylaldehyde.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Williamson:</b> 1&deg; R-X + R&apos;ONa &rarr; R-O-R&apos; + NaX (3&deg; R-X gives Alkene!).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Dehydration Temp:</b> Ethanol &rarr; Ethene (443 K) vs Diethyl ether (413 K).<br><br>\n\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F; font-size: 16px; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">2. Distinction Tests:</b>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Lucas Test:</b> 3&deg; (instant) &gt; 2&deg; (5 min) &gt; 1&deg; (on heating).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Victor Meyer:</b> 1&deg; (Blood Red) | 2&deg; (Blue) | 3&deg; (Colourless).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Cu at 573 K:</b> 1&deg; (Aldehyde) | 2&deg; (Ketone) | 3&deg; (Alkene).<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Phenol Test:</b> Neutral FeCl<sub>3</sub> &rarr; Violet colour; Br<sub>2</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; White precipitate.<br>\n      <b style=\"color: #FF007F;\">&bull; Acidity Order:</b> Picric acid &gt; p-Nitrophenol &gt; o-Nitrophenol &gt; m-Nitrophenol &gt; Phenol &gt; Water &gt; 1&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 3&deg; Alcohol.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>\n";

export const c12Chem7HtmlSolutions = "\n<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #FF007F; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 21px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 12 Chemistry Unit VII: Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers (06 Marks)\n    </h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Master 100 Board Standard Q&amp;A (Section A: 1-40 VSA &bull; Section B: 41-80 SA &bull; Section C: 81-100 LA Derivations)\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q1: Define Alcohols, Phenols, and Ethers.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Alcohols</b> are hydroxy derivatives of aliphatic hydrocarbons (R-OH); <b>Phenols</b> are hydroxy derivatives of aromatic hydrocarbons where -OH is bonded directly to the benzene ring (Ar-OH); <b>Ethers</b> are compounds containing an oxygen atom bonded to two alkyl or aryl groups (R-O-R / Ar-O-R).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q2: Give the systematic IUPAC name of (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-OH.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>2-Methylpropan-2-ol</b> (Common name: tert-butyl alcohol).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q3: Give the systematic IUPAC name of CH<sub>3</sub>-O-CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>2-Methoxypropane</b> (Common name: Isopropyl methyl ether).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q4: Why is the C-O-C bond angle in dimethyl ether (111.7&deg;) greater than the tetrahedral angle (109&deg;28')?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Due to the <b>strong repulsive interaction between the two bulky methyl groups (-CH<sub>3</sub>)</b> attached to the central oxygen atom.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q5: Why is the C-O bond length in phenol (136 pm) shorter than in methanol (142 pm)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because in phenol: (1) The C-O bond acquires <b>partial double bond character</b> due to conjugation of the oxygen lone pair with the benzene ring, and (2) Oxygen is attached to an <b>sp<sup>2</sup> hybridised carbon</b> which is more electronegative than sp<sup>3</sup> carbon.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q6: Why do alcohols have higher boiling points than isomeric ethers and alkanes of comparable molar mass?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because alcohol molecules undergo extensive <b>intermolecular Hydrogen Bonding</b> (&mdash;O-H &bull;&bull;&bull; O&mdash;), which requires significant thermal energy to break.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q7: Why are lower alcohols (methanol, ethanol) readily soluble in water?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because their -OH group forms strong <b>hydrogen bonds with water molecules</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q8: Arrange 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; alcohols in decreasing order of acidic strength and give reason.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 3&deg;</b>. Alkyl groups have electron-releasing (+I) effect. More alkyl groups in 3&deg; alcohols increase electron density on oxygen, destabilizing the alkoxide ion (R-O<sup>-</sup>) and decreasing O-H polarity.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q9: What is Lucas Reagent? State its specific purpose.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">An equimolar mixture of <b>concentrated HCl and anhydrous ZnCl<sub>2</sub></b>; used to distinguish between primary (1&deg;), secondary (2&deg;), and tertiary (3&deg;) alcohols.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q10: How does Lucas reagent distinguish between 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; alcohols at room temperature?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>3&deg; alcohol:</b> Turbidity appears <b>immediately</b>; <b>2&deg; alcohol:</b> Turbidity appears in <b>5 minutes</b>; <b>1&deg; alcohol:</b> Remains clear at room temperature (turbidity appears only on heating).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q11: State the observations in the Victor Meyer test for 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; alcohols.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1&deg; Alcohol:</b> <b>Blood-Red</b> colouration (forms nitrolic acid); <b>2&deg; Alcohol:</b> <b>Deep Blue</b> colouration (forms pseudonitrol); <b>3&deg; Alcohol:</b> <b>Colourless</b> (no reaction). (Trick: <i>Red-Blue-White</i>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q12: What product is obtained when ethanol vapour is passed over heated copper at 573 K?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Ethanal (Acetaldehyde, CH<sub>3</sub>CHO)</b> via catalytic dehydrogenation: CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH &rarr; (Cu / 573 K) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CHO + H<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q13: What product is obtained when 2-methylpropan-2-ol (3&deg; alcohol) vapour is passed over heated Cu at 573 K?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>2-Methylpropene (Isobutylene)</b> via dehydration: (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-OH &rarr; (Cu / 573 K) &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q14: Write the chemical equation for the industrial preparation of methanol from syngas (water gas).\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>CO(g) + 2 H<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; (ZnO - Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> / 573-673 K / 200-300 atm) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>OH(l)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q15: What is Denatured Alcohol (Methylated Spirit)? What substances are added?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Commercial ethanol made unfit for human consumption by adding <b>toxic methanol (5-10%), pyridine (foul odour), and copper sulphate (blue colour)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q16: Distinguish between Rectified Spirit and Absolute Alcohol.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Rectified Spirit</b> contains 95.6% ethanol and 4.4% water (an azeotropic mixture); <b>Absolute Alcohol</b> is 100% pure anhydrous ethanol.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q17: Name the enzyme that converts sucrose into glucose and fructose during fermentation of molasses.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Invertase</b> (C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q18: Name the enzyme that converts glucose into ethanol and carbon dioxide during fermentation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Zymase</b> (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> &rarr; 2 C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 2 CO<sub>2</sub>&uarr;).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q19: Why is phenol significantly more acidic than ethanol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because the conjugate base of phenol, <b>phenoxide ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>)</b>, is resonance-stabilized by delocalizing negative charge over the benzene ring, whereas ethoxide ion (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>) has localized negative charge and is destabilized by the +I effect of ethyl group.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q20: Write the chemical equations for the preparation of phenol from Cumene.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>-O-O-H (Cumene hydroperoxide)</b><br><b>2. Cumene hydroperoxide &rarr; (dil. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH (Phenol) + CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub> (Acetone)</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q21: What is Dow's process for the preparation of phenol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Heating chlorobenzene with 6-8% aqueous NaOH at <b>623 K and 300 atm pressure</b> followed by acidification: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl + 2 NaOH &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + NaCl.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q22: Why does o-nitrophenol have a lower boiling point than p-nitrophenol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because <i>o</i>-nitrophenol exhibits <b>intramolecular hydrogen bonding (chelation)</b> which prevents association between molecules, whereas <i>p</i>-nitrophenol exhibits strong <b>intermolecular hydrogen bonding</b> resulting in extensive molecular association.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q23: Why can a mixture of o-nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol be separated by steam distillation?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><i>o</i>-Nitrophenol is <b>steam volatile</b> due to intramolecular H-bonding, whereas <i>p</i>-nitrophenol is <b>non-steam volatile</b> with a much higher boiling point due to intermolecular H-bonding.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q24: What is Picric acid? Write its IUPAC name.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>2,4,6-Trinitrophenol</b> (prepared by heating phenol with concentrated HNO<sub>3</sub> in conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q25: What is observed when phenol is treated with neutral FeCl<sub>3</sub> solution?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">A characteristic <b>violet / purple colouration</b> appears due to the formation of the complex [Fe(OC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3-</sup>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q26: What is formed when phenol reacts with excess bromine water?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">A <b>white precipitate of 2,4,6-Tribromophenol</b> is formed: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 3 Br<sub>2</sub> &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>2</sub>Br<sub>3</sub>OH&darr; + 3 HBr.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q27: What product is obtained when phenol reacts with Br<sub>2</sub> in non-polar solvent CS<sub>2</sub> at 273 K?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>4-Bromophenol (p-bromophenol)</b> is formed as the major product (and 2-bromophenol as minor).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q28: Write the chemical equation for Kolbe's reaction.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa + CO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (400 K / 4-7 atm) &rarr; 2-Hydroxybenzoic acid (Salicylic acid)</b> (after acidification).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q29: What is the active electrophile in the Reimer-Tiemann reaction?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Dichlorocarbene (:CCl<sub>2</sub>)</b>, a neutral electrophile with a divalent carbon having a sextet of valence electrons.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q30: Write the chemical equation for the Reimer-Tiemann reaction.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + 3 NaOH &rarr; (340 K) &rarr; 2-Hydroxybenzaldehyde (Salicylaldehyde)</b> (after acidification) + 3 NaCl + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q31: What product is formed when phenol is heated with Zinc dust?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Benzene (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>)</b> is formed: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + Zn &rarr; (Heat) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + ZnO.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q32: What product is formed upon oxidation of phenol with acidified Na<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>p-Benzoquinone (Cyclohexa-2,5-diene-1,4-dione)</b>, a conjugated diketone.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q33: State Williamson Ether Synthesis with general equation.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Reaction of a primary alkyl halide with sodium alkoxide via S<sub>N</sub>2 pathway: <b>R-X + R'-O<sup>-</sup>Na<sup>+</sup> &rarr; R-O-R' + NaX</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q34: What happens when tert-butyl bromide is treated with sodium methoxide?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>2-Methylpropene (Isobutylene)</b> is formed exclusively by E2 elimination: (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-Br + CH<sub>3</sub>ONa &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>C=CH<sub>2</sub> + CH<sub>3</sub>OH + NaBr (no ether is formed).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q35: How will you prepare tert-butyl methyl ether using Williamson synthesis?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">By reacting <b>Methyl iodide (1&deg; halide) with Sodium tert-butoxide (3&deg; alkoxide)</b>: CH<sub>3</sub>I + (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O<sup>-</sup>Na<sup>+</sup> &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O-CH<sub>3</sub> + NaI.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q36: Why cannot Diphenyl ether be prepared by Williamson synthesis from bromobenzene and sodium phenoxide?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because aryl halides (bromobenzene) do not undergo nucleophilic substitution (S<sub>N</sub>2) under normal conditions due to partial double bond character of Ar-Br bond and steric repulsion from the &pi;-electron ring.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q37: What products are obtained when ethanol is heated with conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 413 K vs 443 K?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">At <b>413 K:</b> <b>Diethyl ether (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)</b> via intermolecular substitution; at <b>443 K:</b> <b>Ethene (CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub>)</b> via intramolecular elimination.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q38: What products are formed when Anisole (methoxybenzene) is heated with concentrated HI?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Phenol (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH) and Methyl iodide (CH<sub>3</sub>I)</b>: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-CH<sub>3</sub> + HI &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + CH<sub>3</sub>I.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q39: Why does cleavage of anisole with HI yield phenol and methyl iodide instead of iodobenzene and methanol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because the C<sub>phenyl</sub>-O bond has partial double bond character due to resonance and is much stronger than the C<sub>alkyl</sub>-O single bond. Nucleophilic iodide attacks the sp<sup>3</sup> methyl carbon via S<sub>N</sub>2.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q40: What products are obtained when tert-butyl methyl ether is heated with 1 mole of HI?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>tert-Butyl iodide ((CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-I) and Methanol (CH<sub>3</sub>OH)</b> via an S<sub>N</sub>1 mechanism involving a stable tertiary carbocation.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 &amp; 3 Marks each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q41: Explain Hydroboration-Oxidation of propene with chemical equations and specify its regiochemistry.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Propene reacts with diborane (B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>) to form tripropylborane, which on subsequent oxidation with alkaline H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> yields <b>Propan-1-ol</b>:<br><b>6 CH<sub>3</sub>-CH=CH<sub>2</sub> + B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> &rarr; 2 (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>3</sub>B &rarr; (3 H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> / OH<sup>-</sup>) &rarr; 6 CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH + 2 H<sub>3</sub>BO<sub>3</sub></b>.<br>Regiochemistry is <b>overall Anti-Markovnikov addition of water</b> with <b>syn-stereochemistry</b> and zero carbocation rearrangement.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q42: How will you convert Propene into: (i) Propan-1-ol, (ii) Propan-2-ol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Propene &rarr; Propan-1-ol:</b> Hydroboration-Oxidation with B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> followed by alkaline H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub> (Anti-Markovnikov hydration).<br><b>(ii) Propene &rarr; Propan-2-ol:</b> Acid-catalysed hydration by reacting with dilute H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (Markovnikov hydration): CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CH<sub>3</sub>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q43: How can 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; alcohols be synthesized using Grignard reagent (RMgX)? Give general reactions.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Formaldehyde + RMgX &rarr; (H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Primary (1&deg;) Alcohol:</b> HCHO + RMgX &rarr; R-CH<sub>2</sub>OMgX &rarr; R-CH<sub>2</sub>OH.<br><b>2. Other Aldehydes + RMgX &rarr; (H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Secondary (2&deg;) Alcohol:</b> R'CHO + RMgX &rarr; R'-CH(R)OMgX &rarr; R'-CH(OH)-R.<br><b>3. Ketones + RMgX &rarr; (H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Tertiary (3&deg;) Alcohol:</b> R'COR'' + RMgX &rarr; R'R''C(R)OMgX &rarr; R'R''C(OH)R.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q44: Arrange the following in increasing order of acidic strength with explanation: Phenol, p-Nitrophenol, p-Cresol, Ethanol.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Ethanol &lt; p-Cresol &lt; Phenol &lt; p-Nitrophenol</b>.<br><b>Reason:</b> Ethanol is aliphatic (weakest acid, pK<sub>a</sub> &approx; 16). Phenol is much more acidic than ethanol due to resonance-stabilized phenoxide ion (pK<sub>a</sub> &approx; 10). -CH<sub>3</sub> group in <i>p</i>-cresol is electron-donating (+I), which destabilizes phenoxide and lowers acidity. -NO<sub>2</sub> group in <i>p</i>-nitrophenol is strongly electron-withdrawing (-I, -M), which disperses negative charge and maximally stabilizes phenoxide ion (pK<sub>a</sub> = 7.15).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q45: Explain the effect of -NO<sub>2</sub> group on the acidity of phenol at ortho, meta, and para positions.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">The -NO<sub>2</sub> group withdraws electron density by both inductive (-I) and resonance (-M) effects. At <b>ortho and para positions</b>, the -NO<sub>2</sub> group exerts both strong <b>-I and -M effects</b> directly delocalizing the negative charge of the phenoxide ion onto the oxygen atoms of nitro group, making them very strong acids (<i>p</i>-nitrophenol &gt; <i>o</i>-nitrophenol). At <b>meta position</b>, only the <b>-I effect</b> operates (resonance effect -M cannot stabilize negative charge from meta position), so <i>m</i>-nitrophenol is less acidic than <i>o</i>- and <i>p</i>-isomers, but still more acidic than unsubstituted phenol.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q46: Why is p-nitrophenol more acidic than m-nitrophenol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">In <i>p</i>-nitrophenol, the negative charge in phenoxide ion is directly delocalized over the nitro group via resonance (-M effect) as well as inductive effect (-I). In <i>m</i>-nitrophenol, negative charge in canonical structures never falls on the meta-carbon bearing the -NO<sub>2</sub> group, so only the weaker -I effect operates without any resonance stabilization.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q47: Distinguish between the following pairs using simple chemical tests: (i) Ethanol and Propan-2-ol, (ii) Phenol and Ethanol.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Ethanol and Propan-2-ol:</b> Both give yellow precipitate of CHI<sub>3</sub> with I<sub>2</sub>/NaOH (Iodoform test). They can be distinguished by <b>Lucas test</b>: Propan-2-ol (2&deg;) gives turbidity within 5 minutes, whereas Ethanol (1&deg;) gives no turbidity at room temperature.<br><b>(ii) Phenol and Ethanol:</b> Treat with <b>neutral FeCl<sub>3</sub> solution</b>: Phenol gives a characteristic <b>violet colouration</b>, while Ethanol shows no reaction. Alternatively, Bromine water gives a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol with Phenol, but no reaction with Ethanol.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q48: How will you chemically distinguish between Methanol and Ethanol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">By the <b>Iodoform Test</b>: Warm with I<sub>2</sub> and aqueous NaOH.<br><b>Ethanol (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b> possesses the CH<sub>3</sub>-CH(OH)- group and forms a <b>yellow crystalline precipitate of Iodoform (CHI<sub>3</sub>)</b> with a characteristic antiseptic smell.<br><b>Methanol (CH<sub>3</sub>OH)</b> does not possess this group and does not form any precipitate.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q49: Explain the mechanism of Fischer Esterification of an alcohol with a carboxylic acid.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Carboxylic acid reacts with an alcohol in the presence of concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (or dry HCl gas):<br><b>Step 1 (Protonation):</b> Protonation of carboxylic carbonyl oxygen increases electrophilicity of carbonyl carbon.<br><b>Step 2 (Nucleophilic Attack):</b> Oxygen lone pair of alcohol attacks carbonyl carbon to form a tetrahedral intermediate.<br><b>Step 3 (Proton Transfer & Elimination):</b> Proton transfer followed by loss of water molecule and deprotonation yields an <b>Ester (R-CO-OR')</b>.<br><i>Note: The -OR' group of ester comes from the alcohol, and -OH comes from the acid (proved by <sup>18</sup>O isotopic labeling).</i></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q50: How will you convert: (i) Phenol to Aspirin, (ii) Phenol to Salol (Phenyl salicylate)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Phenol to Aspirin:</b> First convert Phenol to Salicylic acid by <b>Kolbe's reaction</b> (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa + CO<sub>2</sub> / 400 K, 4-7 atm &rarr; Salicylic acid). Then acetylate salicylic acid with acetic anhydride in presence of conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>: 2-HOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>COOH + (CH<sub>3</sub>CO)<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; <b>2-CH<sub>3</sub>COOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>COOH (Aspirin)</b> + CH<sub>3</sub>COOH.<br><b>(ii) Phenol to Salol:</b> Heat Salicylic acid with Phenol in the presence of POCl<sub>3</sub>: 2-HOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>COOH + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &rarr; (POCl<sub>3</sub>) &rarr; <b>2-HOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>COOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub> (Salol / Phenyl salicylate)</b> + H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q51: Write the chemical equation, structure, and systematic IUPAC name of Aspirin.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>IUPAC Name:</b> <b>2-Acetoxybenzoic acid</b> (or 2-Ethanoyloxybenzoic acid).<br><b>Equation:</b> Salicylic acid + Acetic anhydride &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Aspirin + Acetic acid.<br><b>Structure:</b> A benzene ring with -COOH at position 1 and -O-COCH<sub>3</sub> at position 2. Used as an analgesic, antipyretic, and anti-inflammatory drug.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q52: Write short notes on: (i) Kolbe's Reaction, (ii) Reimer-Tiemann Reaction.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Kolbe's Reaction:</b> When sodium phenoxide is heated with carbon dioxide (a weak electrophile) at 400 K under 4-7 atm pressure, electrophilic substitution takes place predominantly at the ortho position yielding sodium salicylate, which upon acidification gives <b>Salicylic acid</b>.<br><b>(ii) Reimer-Tiemann Reaction:</b> Heating phenol with chloroform and aqueous NaOH at 340 K introduces an aldehyde (-CHO) group at the ortho position via electrophilic attack by <b>dichlorocarbene (:CCl<sub>2</sub>)</b>, yielding <b>Salicylaldehyde</b> after acidification.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q53: Why does bromination of phenol give 2,4,6-tribromophenol in aqueous medium but 4-bromophenol in CS<sub>2</sub> solvent?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">In <b>aqueous medium (polar solvent)</b>, phenol readily ionizes to form the <b>phenoxide ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>)</b>, which is extraordinarily activated towards electrophilic attack due to full negative charge on oxygen, causing polysubstitution at all 2, 4, 6 positions to give 2,4,6-tribromophenol. In <b>non-polar CS<sub>2</sub> or CHCl<sub>3</sub></b>, phenol remains largely un-ionized as neutral C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH, where the ring activation is moderate, yielding predominantly the monobrominated <b>4-bromophenol</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q54: Write the step-by-step mechanism of cleavage of tert-butyl methyl ether with HI.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Step 1 (Protonation):</b> (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O-CH<sub>3</sub> + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O<sup>+</sup>(H)-CH<sub>3</sub>.<br><b>Step 2 (Carbocation Formation - S<sub>N</sub>1 RDS):</b> The protonated ether undergoes heterolytic cleavage of the C-O bond on the tertiary side to form a very stable <b>tert-butyl carbocation (3&deg;)</b> and neutral CH<sub>3</sub>OH.<br><b>Step 3 (Nucleophilic Attack):</b> (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C<sup>+</sup> + I<sup>-</sup> &rarr; <b>(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-I (tert-Butyl iodide)</b>.<br><b>Overall Products:</b> (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-I + CH<sub>3</sub>OH.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q55: Write the step-by-step mechanism of cleavage of ethyl methyl ether with HI.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Step 1 (Protonation):</b> CH<sub>3</sub>-O-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; CH<sub>3</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>(H)-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>.<br><b>Step 2 (Backside S<sub>N</sub>2 Attack):</b> Iodide ion (I<sup>-</sup>) attacks the less sterically hindered methyl group from the backside, displacing ethanol: I<sup>-</sup> + CH<sub>3</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>(H)-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>I + CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH</b>.<br><b>Overall Products:</b> Methyl iodide + Ethanol.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q56: Explain continuous etherification process for preparing diethyl ether and its limitations.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Heating excess ethanol with concentrated H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at <b>413 K</b>: 2 C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &rarr; (conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> / 413 K) &rarr; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OC<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><b>Limitations:</b> (1) Suitable only for preparing <b>symmetrical primary ethers</b>; (2) Fails for secondary and tertiary alcohols because they undergo elimination to form alkenes; (3) Strict temperature control is vital (at 443 K, ethene is formed instead of ether).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q57: Predict the major products of the following reactions of Anisole: (i) Br<sub>2</sub> in CH<sub>3</sub>COOH, (ii) CH<sub>3</sub>Cl / anhyd. AlCl<sub>3</sub>, (iii) conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> + conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) With Br<sub>2</sub> in CH<sub>3</sub>COOH:</b> <b>4-Bromoanisole (p-bromoanisole)</b> (90% major).<br><b>(ii) With CH<sub>3</sub>Cl / anhyd. AlCl<sub>3</sub>:</b> <b>4-Methoxytoluene (p-methylanisole)</b> (major).<br><b>(iii) With conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> / H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>:</b> <b>4-Nitroanisole (p-nitroanisole)</b> (major).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q58: Why do tertiary alcohols undergo dehydration much more readily than primary alcohols?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because dehydration proceeds via a <b>carbocation intermediate</b> (E1 mechanism). The stability order of carbocations is <b>3&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg;</b> due to hyperconjugation and +I effects. The 3&deg; carbocation forms with the lowest activation energy, making 3&deg; alcohols dehydrate under mild conditions (20% H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub> at 358 K) compared to 1&deg; alcohols (conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 443 K).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q59: How will you convert: (i) Benzene to Phenol, (ii) Chlorobenzene to Phenol, (iii) Aniline to Phenol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Benzene &rarr; Phenol:</b> Benzene + Oleum &rarr; Benzene sulphonic acid &rarr; (NaOH / Fuse at 573 K) &rarr; Sodium phenoxide &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Phenol.<br><b>(ii) Chlorobenzene &rarr; Phenol:</b> Dow's process: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>Cl + 2 NaOH &rarr; (623 K / 300 atm) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa &rarr; (HCl) &rarr; Phenol.<br><b>(iii) Aniline &rarr; Phenol:</b> Diazotisation: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + NaNO<sub>2</sub> + HCl (273-278 K) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> &rarr; (Warm H<sub>2</sub>O) &rarr; Phenol + N<sub>2</sub>&uarr; + HCl.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q60: How will you convert: (i) Ethanol to Propan-2-ol, (ii) Propan-2-ol to 2-Methylpropan-2-ol?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Ethanol &rarr; Propan-2-ol:</b> Oxidize ethanol with PCC to Ethanal (CH<sub>3</sub>CHO). React ethanal with Methylmagnesium bromide (CH<sub>3</sub>MgBr) followed by acidic hydrolysis: CH<sub>3</sub>CHO + CH<sub>3</sub>MgBr &rarr; (H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CH<sub>3</sub>.<br><b>(ii) Propan-2-ol &rarr; 2-Methylpropan-2-ol:</b> Oxidize propan-2-ol with CrO<sub>3</sub> to Propanone (CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>). React propanone with CH<sub>3</sub>MgBr followed by hydrolysis: CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub> + CH<sub>3</sub>MgBr &rarr; (H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-OH.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q61: What happens when Diethyl ether reacts with: (i) Cold concentrated HI, (ii) Excess hot concentrated HI?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) With cold conc. HI:</b> Forms an equimolar mixture of <b>Ethyl iodide and Ethanol</b>: C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OC<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> + HI(cold) &rarr; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>I + C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH.<br><b>(ii) With excess hot conc. HI:</b> Both ethyl groups are converted into <b>Ethyl iodide</b>: C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OC<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> + 2 HI(hot) &rarr; 2 C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>I + H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q62: Why are ethers stored in dark bottles and why are old samples tested for peroxides before distillation?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">On prolonged contact with atmospheric oxygen and light, ethers auto-oxidize to form <b>hazardous organic Peroxides (e.g. Diethyl ether peroxide)</b>. Peroxides are highly unstable and explode violently upon heating during distillation. Dark bottles prevent photochemical oxidation.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q63: How can the presence of peroxides in an ether sample be detected and removed?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Detection:</b> Shake ether with aqueous FeSO<sub>4</sub> solution followed by potassium thiocyanate (KCNS). Peroxides oxidize Fe<sup>2+</sup> to Fe<sup>3+</sup>, giving a <b>blood-red colouration of [Fe(SCN)]<sup>2+</sup></b> with thiocyanate.<br><b>Removal:</b> Distillation after shaking with freshly prepared FeSO<sub>4</sub> solution or solid Cu<sub>2</sub>O reduces peroxides back to ethers.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q64: Explain coupling reaction of phenol with benzene diazonium chloride. Give the reaction and colour of dye.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">In mildly alkaline medium (pH 9-10), phenol reacts with benzene diazonium chloride at 273-278 K via electrophilic aromatic substitution at the para-position to form <b>p-Hydroxyazobenzene (an Orange Azo Dye)</b>:<br><b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>Cl<sup>-</sup> + C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &rarr; (OH<sup>-</sup> / 273-278 K) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-N=N-C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-OH (Orange dye) + Cl<sup>-</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q65: Why is the dipole moment of methanol (1.71 D) higher than that of phenol (1.54 D)?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">In phenol, the C-O bond moment and the O-H bond moment oppose each other because the sp<sup>2</sup> aromatic ring carbon is electron-withdrawing by resonance (-M) and inductive (-I) effects, which partially cancels the net molecular dipole moment. In methanol, the electron-releasing (+I) methyl group reinforces the C-O dipole.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q66: Account for the boiling point order: Butan-1-ol (118 &deg;C) &gt; Butan-2-ol (99.5 &deg;C) &gt; 2-Methylpropan-2-ol (83 &deg;C).\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">These are structural isomers. As branching increases from 1&deg; to 2&deg; to 3&deg;, the molecule becomes more spherical and compact, which reduces its surface area of contact. Consequently, intermolecular van der Waals forces weaken, and steric hindrance also impairs the efficiency of intermolecular hydrogen bonding, lowering boiling points.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q67: Explain the action of: (i) PCl<sub>5</sub> on ethanol, (ii) SOCl<sub>2</sub> on ethanol, (iii) Red P + I<sub>2</sub> on ethanol.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) With PCl<sub>5</sub>:</b> CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH + PCl<sub>5</sub> &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl + POCl<sub>3</sub> + HCl&uarr;</b>.<br><b>(ii) With SOCl<sub>2</sub>:</b> CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH + SOCl<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (Pyridine) &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl + SO<sub>2</sub>&uarr; + HCl&uarr;</b> (Darzen's process, best method).<br><b>(iii) With Red P + I<sub>2</sub>:</b> 3 CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH + PI<sub>3</sub> &rarr; <b>3 CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>I + H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>3</sub></b>.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q68: How will you distinguish between Diethyl ether and Ethanol by physical and chemical tests?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Action with Na metal:</b> Ethanol reacts vigorously with Na liberating H<sub>2</sub> gas with effervescence; Diethyl ether does not react with Na.<br><b>2. Iodoform Test:</b> Ethanol gives a yellow precipitate of CHI<sub>3</sub> with I<sub>2</sub>/NaOH; Diethyl ether gives no reaction.<br><b>3. Boiling Point:</b> Ethanol has a much higher boiling point (78 &deg;C) than diethyl ether (35 &deg;C) due to H-bonding.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q69: Why does sodium phenoxide react with methyl iodide to give anisole, while chlorobenzene does not react with sodium methoxide under normal conditions?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Sodium phenoxide contains nucleophilic phenoxide ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>) which readily attacks methyl iodide via an unhindered S<sub>N</sub>2 backside displacement. In contrast, in chlorobenzene, the C-Cl bond possesses partial double bond character due to resonance and the sp<sup>2</sup> carbon ring repels incoming nucleophiles, resisting S<sub>N</sub>2 substitution.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q70: Give reasons: (i) Alcohols act as both Bronsted acids and Bronsted bases, (ii) Phenol does not undergo substitution with HX like alcohols.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Amphoteric Nature:</b> Alcohols act as <b>Bronsted acids</b> by donating a proton (R-OH &rarr; R-O<sup>-</sup> + H<sup>+</sup>) to strong bases; they act as <b>Bronsted bases</b> by accepting a proton on oxygen lone pairs (R-OH + H<sup>+</sup> &rarr; R-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub>) from strong acids.<br><b>(ii) Phenol vs HX:</b> In phenol, the C-O bond has partial double bond character due to resonance and cannot be easily cleaved by halide ions, unlike the single sp<sup>3</sup> C-O bond in alcohols.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q71: Write the IUPAC names and structures of: (i) Glycerol, (ii) Ethylene glycol, (iii) Catechol, (iv) Resorcinol, (v) Hydroquinone.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Glycerol:</b> Propane-1,2,3-triol (CH<sub>2</sub>OH-CHOH-CH<sub>2</sub>OH).<br><b>(ii) Ethylene glycol:</b> Ethane-1,2-diol (HOCH<sub>2</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>OH).<br><b>(iii) Catechol:</b> Benzene-1,2-diol.<br><b>(iv) Resorcinol:</b> Benzene-1,3-diol.<br><b>(v) Hydroquinone (Quinol):</b> Benzene-1,4-diol.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q72: Convert: (i) Phenol to p-Benzoquinone, (ii) Phenol to 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol, (iii) Phenol to Benzene.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Phenol &rarr; p-Benzoquinone:</b> Oxidation with acidified sodium dichromate: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + Na<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>/H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <i>p</i>-Benzoquinone.<br><b>(ii) Phenol &rarr; 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol:</b> Heating with conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> in conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> yields Picric acid.<br><b>(iii) Phenol &rarr; Benzene:</b> Heating with Zinc dust: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + Zn &rarr; (Heat) &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + ZnO.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q73: An organic compound A (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O) reacts with Na to give H<sub>2</sub>. A on oxidation with PCC gives B (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>O), which on further oxidation gives C (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>4</sub>O<sub>2</sub>). Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Ethanol (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b> (reacts with Na).<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Ethanal (Acetaldehyde, CH<sub>3</sub>CHO)</b> (PCC selectively oxidizes 1&deg; alcohol to aldehyde).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Ethanoic acid (Acetic acid, CH<sub>3</sub>COOH)</b> (further oxidation of aldehyde).</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q74: An organic compound A (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O) does not react with Na. On heating with excess HI, it yields only one alkyl iodide B. Identify A and B.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Since A does not react with Na, it is an ether. Since it yields only one alkyl iodide B upon cleavage, it must be a symmetrical ether.<br><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Diethyl ether (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Ethyl iodide (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>I)</b>.<br><b>Reaction:</b> C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OC<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> + 2 HI &rarr; 2 C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>I + H<sub>2</sub>O.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q75: Why is water a stronger acid than ethanol, but ethanol is a stronger acid than ethyne?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Water (H-OH) is more acidic than ethanol (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH) because the ethyl group (+I effect) pushes electrons toward oxygen in ethoxide ion (C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>), destabilizing it relative to hydroxide ion (OH<sup>-</sup>). Ethanol is more acidic than ethyne because oxygen is much more electronegative than sp-hybridised carbon, stabilizing the alkoxide negative charge far better than acetylide ion.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q76: Explain the role of dry HCl gas in the preparation of esters from alcohols and carboxylic acids.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Dry HCl acts as an acid catalyst and a dehydrating agent (Fischer-Speier esterification). It protonates the carbonyl oxygen of the carboxylic acid, enhancing its electrophilicity, and absorbs the water formed, shifting the equilibrium toward the forward direction according to Le Chatelier's principle.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q77: Why does nitration of phenol with dilute HNO<sub>3</sub> give a low yield of mononitrophenols?\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">Because dilute HNO<sub>3</sub> is an oxidizing agent as well as a nitrating agent. Phenol is highly activated and easily oxidized by HNO<sub>3</sub> into quinones and tarry oxidation products, which lowers the yield of mononitrophenols.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q78: Explain the industrial manufacture of ethanol by fermentation of starchy materials.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\">1. Starch (barley/wheat) is converted into maltose by the enzyme <b>Diastase</b> (from sprouted barley malt): 2 (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>5</sub>)<sub>n</sub> + n H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; n C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub>.<br>2. Yeast is added; <b>Maltase</b> hydrolyzes maltose into glucose: C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>22</sub>O<sub>11</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; 2 C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub>.<br>3. <b>Zymase</b> ferments glucose into ethanol and CO<sub>2</sub>: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O<sub>6</sub> &rarr; 2 C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + 2 CO<sub>2</sub>&uarr;.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q79: Write the chemical equations for Friedel-Crafts alkylation and acylation of Anisole.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Alkylation:</b> Anisole + CH<sub>3</sub>Cl &rarr; (anhyd. AlCl<sub>3</sub>) &rarr; <b>4-Methoxytoluene (major)</b> + 2-methoxytoluene + HCl.<br><b>Acylation:</b> Anisole + CH<sub>3</sub>COCl &rarr; (anhyd. AlCl<sub>3</sub>) &rarr; <b>4-Methoxyacetophenone (major)</b> + 2-methoxyacetophenone + HCl.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q80: Account for: (i) C-O-H bond angle in phenol is 109&deg;, (ii) C-O-C bond angle in ethers is 111.7&deg;.\n    </h3>\n    <p style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) In Phenol:</b> The C-O-H angle (109&deg;) is close to the tetrahedral angle due to lone pair-lone pair repulsions on oxygen.<br><b>(ii) In Ethers:</b> The C-O-C angle (111.7&deg;) is significantly larger than the tetrahedral angle due to <b>steric repulsion between the two bulky alkyl groups</b> attached to oxygen.</p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.12); border-left: 5px solid #FF007F; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 6px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold;\">Section C: Long Answer (LA) Questions &amp; Derivations (5 Marks each)</h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q81: Give the complete step-by-step mechanism for the Acid-Catalysed Dehydration of Ethanol to Ethene at 443 K.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Overall Reaction:</b> CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH &rarr; (conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> / 443 K) &rarr; CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Detailed 3-Step Mechanism:</b><br><b>Step 1: Protonation of Ethanol to form Ethyl Oxonium Ion (Fast Step)</b><br>The unshared electron pair on oxygen attacks H<sup>+</sup> from H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>:<br>CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-OH + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub>.<br><br><b>Step 2: Formation of Ethyl Carbocation (Slowest, Rate-Determining Step - RDS)</b><br>Departure of neutral water molecule from protonated alcohol generates a planar ethyl carbocation:<br>CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (Slow) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>-C<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Step 3: Elimination of a &beta;-Proton to form Ethene (Fast Step)</b><br>Water acts as a base and abstracts a proton from the &beta;-carbon, forming the C=C double bond and regenerating the acid catalyst:<br>H-CH<sub>2</sub>-C<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>.<br><br><b>Ease of Dehydration:</b> 3&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg; (due to carbocation stability order: 3&deg; &gt; 2&deg; &gt; 1&deg;).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q82: Give the complete step-by-step mechanism for the Acid-Catalysed Hydration of Ethene to form Ethanol.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Overall Reaction:</b> CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH.<br><br><b>Detailed 3-Step Mechanism:</b><br><b>Step 1: Electrophilic Attack of Hydronium Ion on Alkene (Slow RDS)</b><br>The &pi;-electrons of ethene attack H<sup>+</sup> of H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> to form an ethyl carbocation:<br>CH<sub>2</sub>=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> &#8652; CH<sub>3</sub>-C<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Step 2: Nucleophilic Attack of Water on Carbocation (Fast Step)</b><br>Water molecule attacks the planar carbocation via its oxygen lone pair, forming a protonated alcohol:<br>CH<sub>3</sub>-C<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O: &#8652; CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub>.<br><br><b>Step 3: Deprotonation to form Ethanol (Fast Step)</b><br>Another water molecule removes a proton from the oxonium ion, yielding neutral ethanol and regenerating H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>:<br>CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &#8652; CH<sub>3</sub>-CH<sub>2</sub>-OH + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>.<br><br>For unsymmetrical alkenes (e.g. propene), the reaction follows <b>Markovnikov's Rule</b> via the more stable carbocation (2&deg; &gt; 1&deg;).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q83: Detail the complete mechanism for the Acid-Catalysed Intermolecular Dehydration of Ethanol to Diethyl Ether at 413 K.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Overall Reaction:</b> 2 C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &rarr; (conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> / 413 K) &rarr; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Detailed S<sub>N</sub>2 Mechanism:</b><br><b>Step 1: Formation of Protonated Ethanol (Ethyl Oxonium Ion)</b><br>C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-OH + H<sup>+</sup> &#8652; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub>.<br><br><b>Step 2: Nucleophilic Backside Attack by Unprotonated Ethanol (S<sub>N</sub>2 RDS)</b><br>A second molecule of ethanol acts as a nucleophile and attacks the &alpha;-carbon of the protonated ethanol with simultaneous displacement of water:<br>C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-OH: + CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>H<sub>2</sub> &rarr; (Slow) &rarr; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>(H)-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Step 3: Deprotonation to yield Diethyl Ether (Fast Step)</b><br>Loss of proton gives diethyl ether:<br>C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O<sup>+</sup>(H)-C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &#8652; C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub> + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>.<br><br><b>Temperature Control:</b> At 413 K, bimolecular substitution (S<sub>N</sub>2) dominates. At 443 K, unimolecular elimination (E1) dominates to give ethene.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q84: Give the complete mechanism for the Reimer-Tiemann Reaction including generation of the intermediate.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Overall Reaction:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + 3 NaOH &rarr; (340 K) &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Salicylaldehyde + 3 NaCl + 2 H<sub>2</sub>O.<br><br><b>Step 1: Generation of Electrophile (Dichlorocarbene :CCl<sub>2</sub>)</b><br>Base (OH<sup>-</sup>) abstracts acidic proton from chloroform: CHCl<sub>3</sub> + OH<sup>-</sup> &#8652; :C<sup>-</sup>Cl<sub>3</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (Loss of Cl<sup>-</sup>) &rarr; <b>:CCl<sub>2</sub> (Dichlorocarbene)</b>.<br><br><b>Step 2: Electrophilic Attack on Phenoxide Ion</b><br>Phenol is deprotonated by base to phenoxide ion, which attacks :CCl<sub>2</sub> at the ortho-position to form a resonance-stabilized carbanion intermediate.<br><br><b>Step 3: Aromatisation and Hydrolysis</b><br>Proton shift restores aromaticity, yielding ortho-(dichloromethyl)phenoxide: -C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>-CH-Cl<sub>2</sub>.<br>Nucleophilic substitution by 2 OH<sup>-</sup> replaces both -Cl atoms with -OH groups, forming an unstable gem-diol -CH(OH)<sub>2</sub>, which spontaneously loses H<sub>2</sub>O to yield <b>Salicylaldehyde (2-hydroxybenzaldehyde)</b> upon acidification.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q85: Describe the complete mechanism and reaction conditions of Kolbe's Reaction for the synthesis of Salicylic acid.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Reaction Conditions:</b> Sodium phenoxide is heated with dry CO<sub>2</sub> gas at <b>400 K</b> under a pressure of <b>4-7 atm</b>, followed by acidification with dilute HCl.<br><br><b>Mechanism:</b><br>1. Phenol is converted to <b>Sodium phenoxide</b> by NaOH. The phenoxide ion has high electron density and strongly activates the ring.<br>2. Carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) is a weak electrophile (O=C=O). The ortho-carbon of phenoxide attacks the central carbon of CO<sub>2</sub>:<br>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup> + CO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; [Intermediate keto-carboxylate] &rarr; (Proton shift) &rarr; Sodium salicylate.<br>3. Acidification with dilute HCl yields pure <b>Salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid)</b>.<br><br><b>Application:</b> Salicylic acid is used to synthesize <b>Aspirin</b> (analgesic) and <b>Methyl salicylate</b> (oil of wintergreen).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q86: Draw the resonance structures of Phenol and Phenoxide ion. Explain why Phenol is acidic and why Phenoxide ion is more stable.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Resonance in Phenol (5 Structures):</b><br>In un-ionized phenol, the lone pair on oxygen is delocalized over the benzene ring, creating positive charge on oxygen and negative charge at ortho/para carbons. These structures involve <b>charge separation</b> (+ and -), which is energetically less favourable.<br><br><b>Resonance in Phenoxide Ion (5 Structures):</b><br>Upon deprotonation, the phenoxide ion (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup>) delocalizes its negative charge across the ring (ortho and para positions). All canonical structures possess a negative charge with <b>NO charge separation</b>.<br><br><b>Conclusion:</b> Since the phenoxide ion is more resonance-stabilized than phenol itself, the ionization equilibrium <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH &#8652; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>O<sup>-</sup> + H<sup>+</sup></b> shifts to the right, giving phenol its distinctly acidic character (pK<sub>a</sub> &approx; 10).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q87: Explain the industrial preparation of Phenol from Cumene (Cumene hydroperoxide process) with mechanisms and significance.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Step 1: Preparation of Cumene (Friedel-Crafts Alkylation)</b><br>Benzene reacts with propene in the presence of H<sub>3</sub>PO<sub>4</sub> (or AlCl<sub>3</sub>) at 523 K: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> (Cumene / Isopropylbenzene)</b>.<br><br><b>Step 2: Aerial Oxidation to Cumene Hydroperoxide</b><br>Cumene is oxidized by passing air or oxygen through it in the presence of slightly alkaline aqueous Na<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>3</sub> (pH 8.5-10.5) at 368-408 K: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>CH(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub> + O<sub>2</sub> &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-C(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>-O-O-H</b>.<br><br><b>Step 3: Acid-Catalysed Cleavage</b><br>Treatment with dilute H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 323-333 K causes Hock rearrangement where phenyl group migrates to electron-deficient oxygen, yielding <b>Phenol (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH) and Acetone (CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>3</sub>)</b>.<br><br><b>Industrial Significance:</b> This single process supplies over 90% of the world's phenol, producing acetone as an equally valuable commercial solvent.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q88: Detail Williamson Ether Synthesis: Reaction mechanism (S<sub>N</sub>2), optimal choice of substrate, limitations, and preparation of mixed ethers.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Mechanism:</b> S<sub>N</sub>2 displacement of halide from an alkyl halide by an alkoxide ion: R'-O<sup>-</sup> + R-X &rarr; [R'...O...C...X] &rarr; R'-O-R + X<sup>-</sup>.<br><br><b>Substrate Selection Rules:</b><br>1. <b>Alkyl Halide (R-X) MUST be 1&deg; (Primary):</b> Backside attack requires minimal steric hindrance.<br>2. <b>Alkoxide Ion (R'O<sup>-</sup>) can be 1&deg;, 2&deg;, or 3&deg;:</b> Alkoxide acts as a strong nucleophile.<br><br><b>Limitations & Traps:</b><br>&bull; If a <b>3&deg; alkyl halide</b> is used with alkoxide (e.g. (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-Br + CH<sub>3</sub>ONa), the strongly basic alkoxide causes <b>E2 elimination</b> giving 2-methylpropene alkene exclusively.<br>&bull; <b>Aryl halides and Vinyl halides</b> cannot be used because C<sub>sp<sup>2</sup></sub>-X bond resists nucleophilic displacement.<br><br><b>Example:</b> To prepare tert-butyl ethyl ether, use <b>Ethyl bromide (1&deg;) + Sodium tert-butoxide (3&deg;)</b>: CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br + (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O<sup>-</sup>Na<sup>+</sup> &rarr; (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + NaBr.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q89: Comprehensive Mechanistic Analysis of C-O Bond Cleavage in Ethers with concentrated HI: S<sub>N</sub>2 vs S<sub>N</sub>1 pathways.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">Cleavage occurs via protonation of ethereal oxygen to form an oxonium ion followed by C-O bond fission:<br><br><b>1. Ethers with Primary and Secondary Alkyl Groups (S<sub>N</sub>2 Pathway):</b><br>Protonated ether is attacked from the backside by iodide ion (I<sup>-</sup>). Because S<sub>N</sub>2 is sensitive to steric hindrance, iodide preferentially attacks the <b>smaller alkyl group</b>.<br><i>Example:</i> CH<sub>3</sub>-O-CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> + HI &rarr; <b>CH<sub>3</sub>I (Methyl iodide) + CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH (Propan-1-ol)</b>.<br><br><b>2. Ethers with Tertiary Alkyl or Benzyl Groups (S<sub>N</sub>1 Pathway):</b><br>Protonated ether undergoes unimolecular heterolysis to form the exceptionally stable <b>tertiary carbocation (3&deg;)</b> or benzyl carbocation. Iodide then attacks the carbocation.<br><i>Example:</i> (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-O-CH<sub>3</sub> + HI &rarr; <b>(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-I (tert-Butyl iodide) + CH<sub>3</sub>OH (Methanol)</b>.<br><br><b>3. Alkyl Aryl Ethers (e.g. Anisole):</b><br>The Ar-O bond has partial double bond character. Attack occurs strictly on the alkyl group: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-CH<sub>3</sub> + HI &rarr; <b>C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + CH<sub>3</sub>I</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q90: Complete comparative study of 1&deg;, 2&deg;, and 3&deg; Alcohols across four diagnostic tests.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Lucas Test (conc. HCl + anhyd. ZnCl<sub>2</sub>):</b><br>&bull; 1&deg; Alcohol: No turbidity at room temperature; appears only on prolonged heating.<br>&bull; 2&deg; Alcohol: Turbidity appears within 5 minutes.<br>&bull; 3&deg; Alcohol: Turbidity appears immediately.<br><br><b>2. Victor Meyer Test:</b><br>&bull; 1&deg; Alcohol: Gives <b>Blood-Red colour</b> with alkali (forms nitrolic acid).<br>&bull; 2&deg; Alcohol: Gives <b>Deep Blue colour</b> with alkali (forms pseudonitrol).<br>&bull; 3&deg; Alcohol: Gives <b>Colourless solution</b>.<br><br><b>3. Heated Copper at 573 K:</b><br>&bull; 1&deg; Alcohol &rarr; Dehydrogenation &rarr; <b>Aldehyde (R-CHO)</b>.<br>&bull; 2&deg; Alcohol &rarr; Dehydrogenation &rarr; <b>Ketone (R-CO-R')</b>.<br>&bull; 3&deg; Alcohol &rarr; Dehydration &rarr; <b>Alkene (Alkene product)</b>.<br><br><b>4. Oxidation with Acidified K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>:</b><br>&bull; 1&deg; Alcohol &rarr; Aldehyde &rarr; Carboxylic acid (same number of C atoms).<br>&bull; 2&deg; Alcohol &rarr; Ketone (same number of C) &rarr; Carboxylic acid (fewer C atoms on vigorous oxidation).<br>&bull; 3&deg; Alcohol &rarr; Resists oxidation; under vigorous conditions gives a mixture of acids with fewer C atoms.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q91: Describe the Electrophilic Aromatic Substitution reactions of Phenol: Halogenation, Nitration, Sulphonation, and Friedel-Crafts.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\">The -OH group strongly activates the benzene ring and directs electrophiles to ortho and para positions:<br><br><b>1. Halogenation:</b><br>&bull; In CS<sub>2</sub> at 273 K: Monobromination gives <b>4-Bromophenol (major)</b>.<br>&bull; In Bromine water: Polysubstitution gives <b>2,4,6-Tribromophenol (white ppt)</b>.<br><br><b>2. Nitration:</b><br>&bull; With dilute HNO<sub>3</sub> at 298 K: Mixture of <b>o-Nitrophenol and p-Nitrophenol</b> (separated by steam distillation).<br>&bull; With conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>/conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>: Forms <b>2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (Picric acid)</b>.<br><br><b>3. Sulphonation:</b><br>&bull; At 288 K: 2-Hydroxybenzenesulphonic acid (o-isomer).<br>&bull; At 373 K: 4-Hydroxybenzenesulphonic acid (p-isomer, thermodynamically stable).<br><br><b>4. Friedel-Crafts Alkylation:</b><br>Phenol + (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>C-Cl / AlCl<sub>3</sub> &rarr; <b>4-tert-Butylphenol (major)</b>.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q92: Describe the Electrophilic Aromatic Substitution reactions of Anisole and explain the directing influence of -OCH<sub>3</sub>.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Directing Influence:</b> The methoxy group (-OCH<sub>3</sub>) has +M resonance effect, increasing electron density at ortho and para positions. Hence, electrophiles attack ortho and para positions (para major due to steric hindrance at ortho):<br><br><b>1. Bromination:</b> Anisole + Br<sub>2</sub> in ethanoic acid &rarr; <b>4-Bromoanisole (90% major)</b> + 2-bromoanisole.<br><br><b>2. Nitration:</b> Anisole + conc. HNO<sub>3</sub>/conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; <b>4-Nitroanisole (major)</b> + 2-nitroanisole.<br><br><b>3. Friedel-Crafts Alkylation:</b> Anisole + CH<sub>3</sub>Cl / anhyd. AlCl<sub>3</sub> &rarr; <b>4-Methoxytoluene (major)</b> + 2-methoxytoluene.<br><br><b>4. Friedel-Crafts Acylation:</b> Anisole + CH<sub>3</sub>COCl / anhyd. AlCl<sub>3</sub> &rarr; <b>4-Methoxyacetophenone (major)</b> + 2-methoxyacetophenone.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q93: Master Chemical Conversions Series 1: (i) Propene &rarr; Propan-1-ol, (ii) Propene &rarr; Propan-2-ol, (iii) 2-Bromopropane &rarr; 1-Bromopropane, (iv) Methanol &rarr; Ethanol, (v) Ethanol &rarr; Methanol.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Propene &rarr; Propan-1-ol:</b> Hydroboration-Oxidation: CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> + B<sub>2</sub>H<sub>6</sub> &rarr; (H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>/OH<sup>-</sup>) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH.<br><b>(ii) Propene &rarr; Propan-2-ol:</b> Acid hydration: CH<sub>3</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub> + H<sub>2</sub>O &rarr; (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH(OH)CH<sub>3</sub>.<br><b>(iii) 2-Bromopropane &rarr; 1-Bromopropane:</b> 1. Dehydrohalogenation with alc. KOH &rarr; Propene. 2. Addition of HBr in presence of peroxide (Kharasch effect) &rarr; 1-Bromopropane.<br><b>(iv) Methanol &rarr; Ethanol:</b> 1. CH<sub>3</sub>OH + PCl<sub>5</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>Cl. 2. CH<sub>3</sub>Cl + KCN &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CN. 3. Reduction with LiAlH<sub>4</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>. 4. Reaction with HNO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH.<br><b>(v) Ethanol &rarr; Methanol:</b> 1. CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH + acidified KMnO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>COOH. 2. CH<sub>3</sub>COOH + NH<sub>3</sub> &rarr; (Heat) &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub>. 3. Hofmann bromamide: CH<sub>3</sub>CONH<sub>2</sub> + Br<sub>2</sub>/KOH &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>. 4. CH<sub>3</sub>NH<sub>2</sub> + HNO<sub>2</sub> &rarr; CH<sub>3</sub>OH.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q94: Master Chemical Conversions Series 2: (i) Phenol &rarr; Aspirin, (ii) Phenol &rarr; Salicylaldehyde, (iii) Phenol &rarr; Picric acid, (iv) Phenol &rarr; Anisole, (v) Phenol &rarr; Phenolphthalein.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>(i) Phenol &rarr; Aspirin:</b> Phenol + NaOH &rarr; Sodium phenoxide &rarr; (CO<sub>2</sub> / 400 K, 4-7 atm) &rarr; Salicylic acid &rarr; ((CH<sub>3</sub>CO)<sub>2</sub>O / H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Aspirin.<br><b>(ii) Phenol &rarr; Salicylaldehyde:</b> Reimer-Tiemann: Phenol + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + 3 NaOH (340 K) &rarr; (H<sup>+</sup>) &rarr; Salicylaldehyde.<br><b>(iii) Phenol &rarr; Picric acid:</b> Phenol + conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> (sulphonation) then conc. HNO<sub>3</sub> &rarr; 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol.<br><b>(iv) Phenol &rarr; Anisole:</b> Phenol + NaOH &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>ONa + CH<sub>3</sub>I &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-CH<sub>3</sub> + NaI.<br><b>(v) Phenol &rarr; Phenolphthalein:</b> Heat 2 molecules of Phenol with Phthalic anhydride in presence of conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> &rarr; Phenolphthalein + H<sub>2</sub>O.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q95: Multi-Step Roadmap 1: Primary alcohol A (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O) on oxidation gives B (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O<sub>2</sub>). A with SOCl<sub>2</sub> gives C (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>9</sub>Cl). C with alc. KOH gives D (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>8</sub>), which with HBr/peroxide gives E (C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>9Br</sub>), an isomer of C. Identify A to E.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Butan-1-ol (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH)</b> (1&deg; alcohol).<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Butanoic acid (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>COOH)</b> (oxidation of 1&deg; alcohol).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>1-Chlorobutane (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Cl)</b> (reaction of A with SOCl<sub>2</sub>).<br><b>Compound D:</b> <b>But-1-ene (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub>)</b> (dehydrohalogenation of C with alc. KOH).<br><b>Compound E:</b> <b>1-Bromobutane (CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Br)</b> (Anti-Markovnikov addition of HBr to D in presence of peroxide).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q96: Multi-Step Roadmap 2: Aromatic compound A (C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O) is insoluble in water and aq. NaOH. A reacts with hot conc. HI to give B (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O) and C (CH<sub>3</sub>I). B gives violet colour with neutral FeCl<sub>3</sub> and on heating with Zn dust gives D (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>). Identify A, B, C, D.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Anisole (Methoxybenzene, C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>-O-CH<sub>3</sub>)</b> (insoluble in aq. NaOH because it is an ether).<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Phenol (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH)</b> (cleavage product of anisole, gives violet colour with neutral FeCl<sub>3</sub>).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>Methyl iodide (CH<sub>3</sub>I)</b>.<br><b>Compound D:</b> <b>Benzene (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>)</b> (reduction of phenol B with Zn dust).<br><b>Equations:</b> C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OCH<sub>3</sub> + HI &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + CH<sub>3</sub>I; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + Zn &rarr; C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub> + ZnO.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q97: Multi-Step Roadmap 3: Organic compound A (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O) gives violet colour with FeCl<sub>3</sub>. With CHCl<sub>3</sub>/NaOH, it forms B and C (C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>6</sub>O<sub>2</sub>). B is steam volatile and on reduction with Zn/HCl gives 2-methylphenol. Identify A, B, C.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Compound A:</b> <b>Phenol (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH)</b>.<br><b>Compound B:</b> <b>Salicylaldehyde (2-Hydroxybenzaldehyde)</b> (ortho-isomer, steam volatile due to intramolecular H-bonding).<br><b>Compound C:</b> <b>4-Hydroxybenzaldehyde</b> (para-isomer, non-steam volatile).<br><b>Reaction:</b> Reimer-Tiemann reaction: C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH + CHCl<sub>3</sub> + NaOH &rarr; Salicylaldehyde + 4-Hydroxybenzaldehyde.<br>Reduction of B: 2-HOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>CHO + 4 [H] &rarr; (Zn/HCl) &rarr; 2-HOC<sub>6</sub>H<sub>4</sub>CH<sub>3</sub> (2-methylphenol) + H<sub>2</sub>O.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q98: Commercial preparation, properties, and biological significance of Methanol and Ethanol.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Methanol (Wood Spirit, CH<sub>3</sub>OH):</b><br>Manufactured by catalytic reduction of CO with H<sub>2</sub> over ZnO-Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> at 573-673 K and 200-300 atm. Used as an industrial solvent and for making formaldehyde. <b>Biological Toxicity:</b> Ingested methanol is oxidized to methanal and methanoic acid, which damages retinal cells causing permanent blindness and fatal acidosis.<br><br><b>2. Ethanol (Grain Alcohol, C<sub>2</sub>H<sub>5</sub>OH):</b><br>Manufactured by fermentation of molasses/sugar: Sucrose &rarr; (Invertase) &rarr; Glucose + Fructose &rarr; (Zymase) &rarr; Ethanol + CO<sub>2</sub>. <b>Denaturation:</b> Denatured alcohol contains 5% methanol and pyridine to prevent human consumption. Used as an antiseptic, solvent, beverage, and clean fuel additive (gasohol).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q99: Systematic investigation of Acidic Strength of substituted Phenols with pK<sub>a</sub> values and electronic effects.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>Quantitative pK<sub>a</sub> Data:</b><br>&bull; 2,4,6-Trinitrophenol (Picric acid): <b>pK<sub>a</sub> = 0.78</b> (extremely strong acid due to three -NO<sub>2</sub> groups exerting -I and -M effects).<br>&bull; 4-Nitrophenol: <b>pK<sub>a</sub> = 7.15</b>.<br>&bull; 2-Nitrophenol: <b>pK<sub>a</sub> = 7.23</b> (slightly weaker than 4-nitrophenol due to intramolecular H-bonding with -OH).<br>&bull; 3-Nitrophenol: <b>pK<sub>a</sub> = 8.35</b> (only -I effect operates, no resonance -M stabilization).<br>&bull; Phenol: <b>pK<sub>a</sub> = 10.00</b>.<br>&bull; 4-Methylphenol (p-Cresol): <b>pK<sub>a</sub> = 10.26</b> (+I and hyperconjugation destabilize phenoxide ion).<br>&bull; Ethanol: <b>pK<sub>a</sub> = 16.00</b>.<br><b>Conclusion:</b> Electron-withdrawing substituents (-NO<sub>2</sub>, -CN, -Cl) stabilize phenoxide ion and decrease pK<sub>a</sub> (increase acidity); electron-donating substituents (-CH<sub>3</sub>, -OCH<sub>3</sub>) destabilize phenoxide and increase pK<sub>a</sub> (decrease acidity).</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(255, 0, 127, 0.25); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #FF007F; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Q100: Synthesis, Structure, Identification, and Chemical Reactivity Roadmap of Ethers.\n    </h3>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 15.5px;\"><b>1. Synthesis:</b><br>&bull; Intermolecular dehydration of 1&deg; alcohols with conc. H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub> at 413 K.<br>&bull; Williamson Ether Synthesis: 1&deg; R-X + R'O<sup>-</sup>Na<sup>+</sup> &rarr; R-O-R' + NaX (S<sub>N</sub>2).<br><br><b>2. Structure & Physical Properties:</b><br>Bent geometry with C-O-C angle = 111.7&deg; (steric repulsion of alkyl groups). Weak dipole moment (&mu; &approx; 1.15-1.3 D). Low boiling points due to lack of H-bonding; soluble in water via H-bonding.<br><br><b>3. Chemical Reactivity:</b><br>&bull; <b>Cleavage by HI:</b> S<sub>N</sub>2 with 1&deg;/2&deg; alkyls (iodide attacks smaller group); S<sub>N</sub>1 with 3&deg; alkyls (iodide attacks 3&deg; carbocation).<br>&bull; <b>Anisole Cleavage:</b> Yields phenol and methyl iodide exclusively.<br>&bull; <b>Electrophilic Substitution:</b> -OCH<sub>3</sub> group is ortho/para-directing (bromination, nitration, Friedel-Crafts).<br>&bull; <b>Peroxide Hazard:</b> Auto-oxidation by O<sub>2</sub> forms explosive peroxides, detected by FeSO<sub>4</sub>/KCNS test.</div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n";

